{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3b1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a07060-dc53-7421-8823-10572f5f580f"},"sequence":51,"recorded_at":1780531400000097,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"1bd0f4b7-3d42-45e7-aae9-47d49f725647","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"1bd0f4b7-3d42-45e7-aae9-47d49f725647","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"1bd0f4b7-3d42-45e7-aae9-47d49f725647"},"call_id":"call_01a07060f531762388cca902074ba9c8","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/fable-consolidated-review.md`.\n1|# Consolidated cross-Story review: terminal-backend-registry x provider-plugin-host\n2|\n3|Reviewer: outside-eye consolidated pass (Fable), 2026-09-05.\n4|Scope: the union of two unlanded Story branches against `main` @ `57afcc6`.\n5|\n6|- `STORY-260830-3m2mw8` (`.temp/STORY-260830-3m2mw8/worktree`, tip `adba01f` + uncommitted leaf 3): `internal/terminalbackend` (3,597 prod LOC, 6,635 test LOC, 93 test funcs), plus `internal/config` and `internal/traceability` edits.\n7|- `STORY-260830-3jqsx1` (`.temp/STORY-260830-3jqsx1/worktree`, tip `8fbc052` + uncommitted leaf 3): `internal/provider` (663 prod / 2,872 test / 54 funcs), `internal/provhost` (3,082 prod / 6,301 test / 117 funcs), plus a CI edit.\n8|\n9|Method: full read of every production file in the three new packages; selective read of the inventory/census/conformance tests; comparison against the shared baseline packages (`scalar`, `canonicaljson`, `axerror`, `catalog`, `specpin`, `specdoc`, `traceability`), which are byte-identical across the two worktrees; spec checks against the pinned `internal/specdoc/SPEC.md` (v0.5.0, pin `562546d2…`). One empirical probe (Go JSON lone-surrogate decode) run in scratchpad. No file in either worktree was modified. Mutation batteries were not re-run.\n10|\n11|Change-set overlap between the branches relative to main: only `README.md` and `LOGBOOK.md` (both modified in both). Zero production-package overlap.\n12|\n13|---\n14|\n15|## Findings (ordered by severity)\n16|\n17|### B1 — BLOCKING (story 1): the §7.A descriptor check enforces 3 of the 4 dimensions the MUST names; the missing one is unrepresentable\n18|\n19|SPEC.md:3473-3475: \"The Provider MUST reject a descriptor whose **binding digest**, backend ID/version, or generation does not match the AX-validated host-local binding before launching or observing a provider process.\"\n20|\n21|- `internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go:557` — `InstanceBinding` has exactly `BackendID`, `ImplementationVersion`, `ProtocolVersion`, `Generation`. There is no binding-digest member, so `CheckProviderDescriptor` (`terminalbackend.go:568`) cannot check it, ever.\n22|- The doc comment on `CheckProviderDescriptor` quotes the spec sentence with the binding-digest clause elided: \"the provider rejects a descriptor whose backend ID, versions, or generation do not match…\". The self-description is accurate about what the function sees and silent about the enumerated dimension it dropped — the exact recurring defect shape named in the review brief.\n23|- All leaf tests (`terminalbackend_test.go:754-855`) drive ID/version/protocol/generation mismatches only; `terminal_binding_id` appears nowhere in the package outside the (unrelated) evidence realm member. Grep proof: `grep -rn \"binding digest\" internal/terminalbackend/` matches nothing.\n24|\n25|Ratio: 3/4 mandated match dimensions implemented; 0 tests on the fourth.\n26|\n27|Close by: add `TerminalBindingID` (digest of the validated Terminal Instance Binding 1.0.0, SPEC.md:3461) to `InstanceBinding`, check equality in `CheckProviderDescriptor` with its own refusal arm and negative test, or record an explicit stated bound in the package doc plus a traceability gap entry saying the digest dimension is deferred and to which task.\n28|\n29|### B2 — BLOCKING (story 1): string bounds counted in bytes where the spec defines UTF-8 characters; the two Stories disagree on the unit of the same shared member\n30|\n31|SPEC.md:321: \"`string[n..m]` bounds UTF-8 characters.\" SPEC.md:1034: `backend_generation` — \"string[1..256]; this is the canonical bound in every containing contract.\" SPEC.md:1042-1044 mandates boundary conformance: reject lengths 0 and 257, accept length 256.\n32|\n33|- `internal/terminalbackend/terminalbackend.go:592` (`checkGeneration`) and `manifest.go:1328` (`GenerationDigest`) enforce `len(generation) <= 256` — **bytes** — and say so: \"string[1..256] measured in bytes over valid UTF-8\".\n34|- `internal/terminalbackend/manifest.go:379` (`boundedStringMember`, used for `os_version`, `provider_build`) — same byte counting, with the comment \"the canonical bound in every containing contract\" attached to the wrong unit.\n35|- The repo baseline counts runes: `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go:1879` (`requireBoundedString`, `utf8.RuneCountInString`). Story 2 counts runes: `internal/provhost/opdecode.go:154` (`runeLength`, with the correct rationale in its comment).\n36|\n37|Consequence: a 200-character generation of 2-byte runes (400 bytes) is spec-valid, accepted by the provhost-side reading, and refused by every terminalbackend gate (`GenerationDigest`, `checkGeneration`, probe generation binding). The divergence is over-strict, never over-permissive, so nothing invalid is admitted — but the same `backend_generation` value crosses both protocols (§4.B binding, §7.A descriptor), and the two Stories will disagree about its validity at the first composition. Four review rounds could not see this because every boundary fixture in the leaf tests is ASCII, where bytes == runes.\n38|\n39|Close by: switch `checkGeneration`, `GenerationDigest`, and `boundedStringMember` to `utf8.RuneCountInString`, keep the `utf8.ValidString` precondition, and add one multibyte boundary fixture on each side of 256 (256 two-byte runes accept; 257 reject). Note `terminal_backend_id` is unaffected — the spec defines it in ASCII bytes explicitly.\n40|\n41|### B3 — BLOCKING (both stories): both hand-rolled strict decoders admit lone-surrogate escapes the spec MUST-rejects; terminalbackend canonicalizes such documents\n42|\n43|SPEC.md:289: \"Decoders MUST reject lone surrogate code points before canonicalization.\" The repo's owner of this rule exists and is enforced: `internal/canonicaljson/canonical.go:401` calls `validateSurrogateEscapes` (defined at `canonical.go:514`) inside `decodeStrict`.\n44|\n45|Both Stories re-implemented strict object decoding instead of reusing it, and both re-implementations are blind to exactly this enumerated class:\n46|\n47|- `internal/terminalbackend/manifest.go:273` (`decodeStrictObject`): checks UTF-8, duplicate keys, depth, trailing data — no surrogate check. Go's `encoding/json` silently replaces a lone `\\ud800` escape with U+FFFD (verified empirically: `json.Unmarshal([]byte(\"\\\"\\\\ud800\\\"\"))` yields `\"\\ufffd\"`, no error). A Manifest/Probe/Evidence whose `os_version` is `\"\\ud800abc\"` decodes to a valid UTF-8 string, passes `boundedStringMember`, and the document is **accepted** — after `checkIdentity` (`manifest.go:537`) has already run `jcs.Transform` over it. That is canonicalization of a document containing a lone surrogate, i.e. the MUST violated in both halves (no rejection, and canonicalization performed).\n48|- `internal/provhost/protocol.go:262` (`decodeStrictObject`): same gap. A SpawnPlan, manifest `display_name`, identity `native_session_id`, etc. carrying a lone-surrogate escape is admitted after replacement. provhost never canonicalizes (identity recomputation is explicitly deferred), so the violation is of the decoder half only, but the wire bodies are Section-1.6 JSON and the divergence from `canonicaljson.decodeStrict` is the same class.\n49|\n50|This is the brief's recurring defect at cross-Story scale: two independent strict decoders, each accurate about what it checks, both blind to the same class the canonical package enumerates — and neither leaf reviewer could see it because the strong decoder lives in a package neither Story touched.\n51|\n52|Close by: extract the surrogate gate (or the whole strict-decode front end) from `canonicaljson` into a reusable entry point and call it from both `decodeStrictObject`s; add one `\\ud800` fixture per package proving refusal. A local copy of `validateSurrogateEscapes` is acceptable if export is unwanted, but then B-directional tests should pin the two copies equal.\n53|\n54|### B4 — BLOCKING (story 2, at integration): ~3,700 production lines land invisible to the spec-to-code traceability gate, under gap prose that asserts they do not exist\n55|\n56|- `internal/traceability/ownership.v0.5.0.json` (story 2 worktree = main's version) line 2776 gap text: \"…**no provider manifest is implemented anywhere in this repository**.\" Line 2731: the Section 5.5 destination-adapter gap. `internal/provhost/manifest.go` (`DecodeManifest`) and `identity.go` (`CheckIdentity`) now implement exactly those surfaces; story 2 changes neither the ownership registry nor the reviewed hash (`internal/traceability/traceability.go:43`, `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`).\n57|- `tracecheck` verifies registry -> code only (`traceability.go:308` `verifyOwnership`, `traceability.go:868` `sourceChecker.verify`); nothing scans code -> registry, so an entire unregistered package keeps the gate green. Story 2's tests pass today for precisely that reason. This is the defect family's instance in the meta-gate itself: the census enumerates what registered itself.\n58|- Story 1 did update the registry for its own code (`ownership.v0.5.0.json` +116 lines, `terminal-manifest-probe-admission` group) — but registered nothing for `CheckProviderDescriptor`, so §7.A still carries only the catalog binding with \"no clause of 7.A is enumerated\" (story-1 worktree, `ownership.v0.5.0.json:2960-2970`) while §7.A production code now exists in the same branch.\n59|\n60|Close by (before or at landing of story 2): add ownership groups for `internal/provider` and `internal/provhost`, rewrite the now-false §5.5/§7.2/§7.3/§7.5 gap texts, re-pin `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`; story 1 should either register its §7.A code or state the deferral in the §7.A gap. Longer-term (advisory): give tracecheck a reverse direction — e.g. require every `internal/*` package to appear in at least one ownership row or an explicit exclusion list — so the next unregistered package cannot land silently.\n61|\n62|---\n63|\n64|### A1 — ADVISORY (structural, needs a decision before the next story): two identity/shape pipelines now exist for the same three terminal schemas\n65|\n66|`canonicaljson` is the designated owner of object identity: it has the trusted schema->self-field contract table (validated against the generated catalog, `canonical.go:103-152`, with `SelfManifestID`/`SelfProbeID`/`SelfEvidenceID` at `canonical.go:56,70,72`) and registers all three terminal schemas — currently as refuse-everything placeholders (`closed_shapes.go:115,130,132`). Story 1 built a second, private pipeline: `objectIdentity`/`checkIdentity` (`manifest.go:515,537`) plus full shape validation, bypassing `canonicaljson`'s strict decode (B3), AX safe-integer gate, and 5 MiB bound.\n67|\n68|Today the two owners give opposite verdicts on every valid terminal manifest: `terminalbackend.ParseManifest` accepts what `canonicaljson.CalculateObjectIdentity` refuses (\"unsupported\"). Whichever future code path reaches for the repo's documented identity entry point will disagree with the registry. Also note the safe-integer ordering: `checkIdentity` runs `jcs.Transform` (which rounds unsafe integers, SPEC.md:302 NUM-UNSAFE-ROUND) *before* member-type validation refuses the document — no wrong accept is reachable, but rejection happens after identity calculation, which the fixture wording forbids.\n69|\n70|Close by: one owner. Either implement the three closed shapes inside `canonicaljson` and have `terminalbackend` parse via `VerifyObjectIdentity`, or formally transfer ownership (remove/redirect the placeholder registrations and record it in the ownership registry). Do not leave both.\n71|\n72|### A2 — ADVISORY: `provhost.CheckIdentity` duplicates `canonicaljson.validateProviderIdentityRecord` rule-for-rule\n73|\n74|`internal/provhost/identity.go:90` vs `internal/canonicaljson/core_records.go:207`: same 16-member set, same UUIDv7/subject==session rules, same bounds (1..128/1..256/1..512), same kind enum, same Antigravity realm rule, same opaque-map rules, byte-identical regexes (`identityKeyPattern` == `providerIdentityKeyPattern`, `windowsDrivePattern` == `windowsAbsolutePathPattern`). They already differ in decoder strictness (B3) and in error surface (provider_protocol_error vs identity error). provhost additionally skips `record_id` recomputation with a stated bound (\"content addressing belongs to the object-identity story\") — the correct deferral — but then re-validates everything else instead of delegating. Two validators for `urn:ax:schema:provider-identity 1.0.0` will drift.\n75|\n76|Close by: have `CheckIdentity` delegate the record-shape half to `canonicaljson` (mapping its error into the §15.1 provider-stdio refusal), keeping only the wants-this-provider check local; or add a bidirectional agreement test driving both validators over one fixture corpus.\n77|\n78|### A3 — ADVISORY: Provider Protocol 3.0.0 is pinned into v0.5.0 and no package claims it — the union has no owner for the §7.A envelope\n79|\n80|`internal/catalog/catalog_gen.go:190-204`: every provider operation carries `ContractVersions: [\"2.0.0\", \"3.0.0\"]`, `NormativeSection: \"7.2, 7.5, 7.A\"`, both released in v0.5.0. `internal/provhost/protocol.go:30` pins `ProtocolMajor = 2` and `protocol.go:449` classifies any 3.x envelope as `incompatible_protocol` (correct for a v2 call). §7.A binds Structured Error **1.3.0** for v3; `protocol.go:381` binds major 2 -> Error 1.0.0 only. provhost's otherwise meticulous stated-bounds paragraph (`doc.go`) never mentions v3. The only §7.A production code in either branch is `terminalbackend.CheckProviderDescriptor` — in the terminal package, incomplete (B1), and with no possible caller in provhost.\n81|\n82|This is the \"two independently excellent packages that disagree with each other\" case: story 1 believes §7.A is its scope (package doc says so); story 2 built the only component that could ever speak v3 and scoped it out silently.\n83|\n84|Close by: a decision, not necessarily code — name the owner of the v3 envelope (Error 1.3.0 binding, descriptor carriage in launch/resume bodies), record it in provhost's stated bounds and the ownership registry, and move/complete `CheckProviderDescriptor` accordingly.\n85|\n86|### A4 — ADVISORY: three error models; `internal/provider`'s cannot compose into the wire layer as built\n87|\n88|- provhost mints wire-ready `axerror` Structured Errors, dynamic facts only in `Details` — matches `axerror`'s gates.\n89|- terminalbackend mints its package `*Error` with catalog wire codes and static details — composable.\n90|- provider mints its own `Error` whose `detail` deliberately embeds canonical filesystem paths, provider IDs, and owner identities (`provider.go:77-97`, `trustCandidate`, `Verify`), and whose `Error()` appends `cause.Error()`. The repo convention everywhere else (scalar `ValidationError` — \"without echoing the input, which may be a machine-local path\"; terminalbackend; provhost) is the opposite. When `cmd/ax` lifts a discovery failure into a Structured Error, a message built from `provider.Error.Error()` with the cause attached will be refused by `axerror.refuseCausalLeak` (`details.go:202-215` — message reproduces the rendered cause verbatim), so the seam will not compose without rework. Not shown to violate §15.1 itself (its excluded classes are env/stderr/bundle keys), so advisory.\n91|\n92|Close by: either restructure provider's constructors to carry paths in a details map (provhost pattern) now, or record the composition contract (who strips the cause, who builds the wire message) in provider's doc before `cmd/ax` exists.\n93|\n94|### A5 — ADVISORY: the six-provider set exists as two hand-written literals bridged only by the spec pin\n95|\n96|`internal/provider/provider.go` `builtinOrder` (derived-checked against §7.1 lines 2622-2653 by `enumeration_test.go:63`) and `internal/provhost/profile.go` `profileProviders` (derived-checked against the §7.7 table lines 3431-3436 by `closed_vocabulary_census_test.go:367`). While the pin holds, green suites cannot diverge — the agreement is real but transitive through SPEC.md line windows. No test asserts `provider.Builtins()` equals provhost's registry directly, and provhost's census explicitly scopes `profileProviders` out (\"nothing in production consults it\" — `ProfileMapping` consults `profileYOLOMapping` instead). One cross-package equality test at integration closes this structurally for the next spec bump.\n97|\n98|### A6 — ADVISORY: inventory machinery is now 20 hand-rolled AST walkers across 10 packages; these two Stories added 7\n99|\n100|Files containing `go/parser` in test code: 20 (10 in canonicaljson/cliresult/config/localstore/specdoc pre-existing; provider adds 3, provhost adds 3, terminalbackend adds 1). Three distinct architectures for the same idea:\n101|\n102|- terminalbackend (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go`): derived arms vs declared rows, resolution is **textual** (a row passes if the named test's source mentions the detail); self-documents that a dead arm can resolve through a sibling.\n103|- provhost (`refusal_arm_inventory_test.go:73-165`, floor 162 arms): derived arms vs witnesses, and `TestEveryArmWitnessRefusesAtTheProductionEntry` **executes** every witness through the production entry point and requires the refusal to come from that arm.\n104|- provider (`refusal_inventory_test.go:31-62`): runtime instrumentation — constructor vars swapped in `TestMain`, exercised file:line set audited against AST-derived sites.\n105|\n106|Two consequences. First, the weaker bound is the real bound: story 1's textual resolution proves declaration, not exercise; the executed-witness direction provhost built is strictly stronger and portable. Second, each walker re-decides \"what is a production file / what counts as a refusal\" independently, and each closes a different blind-spot set (terminalbackend rejects constructor aliasing outright; provider catches aliases at runtime; provhost's ctor-derivation keys on identifier names and relies on its separate call-site audit for aliases). Recommend extracting a shared test-support core (production-file glob + fail-closed parse + both-direction check harness) before an 8th copy appears, and porting executed witnesses to terminalbackend.\n107|\n108|### A7 — ADVISORY: two trust-establishment paths for external executables, with different fact sets, and nothing cross-checks them\n109|\n110|`terminalbackend.DigestFile` (`terminalbackend.go:603`): EvalSymlinks + regular-file + sha256, no owner check (§6.5 trust = digest + enabled). `provider.trustCandidate` (`provider.go:394`): canonicalize + inspect + **owner policy** + digest, plus re-verification (`Verify`, constant-time compare). If §6.5 genuinely has no owner-approval dimension while §7.1 does, fine — but no test or comment on the terminal side cites that asymmetry, and the symlink/regular-file semantics are near-duplicates that can drift. Worth one comment citing the §6.5/§7.1 difference and, if the spec is silent, a deliberate decision.\n111|\n112|### A8 — ADVISORY: integration logistics\n113|\n114|- `README.md` and `LOGBOOK.md` are modified by both branches: guaranteed textual merge conflict; whoever lands second must merge, not overwrite.\n115|- Story 1 rewired `internal/config/validation.go` to import `terminalbackend.ParseID` (the right deduplication direction — the model for A1/A2) and re-pinned `reviewedOwnershipCanonicalSHA256`; story 2 edits `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (adds `GOOS=windows go vet`). No production conflict, but B4's registry work will touch the same pin story 1 just moved — land story 1 first or coordinate the hash.\n116|- The identical SemVer regex now exists in 7 production files in the union (specpin, config, canonicaljson, cliresult, axerror + one per new package). Pre-existing idiom, both Stories followed it; a shared `scalar` semver would retire 7 copies.\n117|\n118|### A9 — ADVISORY (nits)\n119|\n120|- `terminalbackend.ParseID` (`terminalbackend.go:130-140`) puts the raw grammar-refused input (arbitrary UTF-8, up to 128 bytes) into `Error.BackendID`, which `Error()` prints — at odds with the package's \"never echoes local data\" posture. Bound it or omit it on the grammar arm.\n121|- `provhost.parseMajor` (`protocol.go` — used only to classify foreign frames) accepts leading zeros (\"03.0.0\" -> major 3, classified as mismatch). Harmless — classification only — but stricter-than-semver elsewhere, looser here.\n122|- `provhost.RequireCapability` re-decodes the probe body three times discarding faults after `DecodeProbe` validated it — correct but fragile against future reordering; a comment or a parsed-probe return from `DecodeProbe` would remove the coupling.\n123|\n124|---\n125|\n126|## Checked and found sound\n127|\n128|- **Wire-code membership**: every code minted by all three packages is in the pinned catalog — `terminal_backend_*` (incl. `terminal_backend_integrity_failure` at Error 1.3.0, `catalog_gen.go:545`), `invalid_config` / `local_precondition_failed` / `integrity_failure` / `provider_timeout` / `provider_protocol_error` / `provider_process_failed` / `incompatible_protocol` / `idempotency_mismatch` (`catalog_gen.go:445-479`).\n129|- **Grammar agreement where duplicated**: provhost's `providerIDPattern` == `scalar`'s; semver regexes identical everywhere; provhost's hand-written platform vocabularies equal `scalar`'s set and are census-derived from the spec; terminalbackend routes platforms through `scalar.ParsePlatform`.\n130|- **Digest convention**: everything flows through `scalar.ParseDigest`/`SHA256Digest`; `DigestFile`'s `\"sha256:\"+hex` output parses; `provider.Verify` compares constant-time.\n131|- **Version majors**: Terminal Backend Protocol major 1 (story 1) vs Provider Protocol major 2 (story 2) vs Configuration 3.0.0 — consistent with catalog contract pins; the two Kind vocabularies (`builtin_go/local_program/trusted_executable/native_runtime` §4.B vs `builtin/external` §7.1) are distinct spec vocabularies, not a divergence.\n132|- **Spec pin**: `specpin`, `specdoc`, `axerror`, `scalar`, `canonicaljson`, `catalog` are byte-identical across the two worktrees; both Stories derive vocabularies from the same `SPEC.md` bytes, so all census/derivation tests measure the same document.\n133|- **provhost transport** (`runner.go`): drain-before-wait pipe ownership, process-group kill with `WaitDelay`, capped reads sized so length checks stay exact, EPIPE-race handling that prefers a judgeable frame over a write error — read closely, no defect found.\n134|- **terminalbackend Reconcile** (`manifest.go:1511-1859`): deterministic check order, dedup-before-signature is safe (first instance verified, equal-members required), evidence tuple/liveness/coverage/ID-set rules internally consistent; `CheckAttachRequest` matches the §4.C \"valid unexpired\" rule (no not-before exists in the spec row — checked).\n135|- **provider Discover**: deterministic ordering (sorted entries within dirs, listed dir order, builtin order, PATH order), fail-closed on every read failure, duplicate refusal before probe by construction.\n136|- The task-board resource/mutation numbers were not re-derived; I relied on both trees' stated green `go test ./...` and did not re-run the mutation batteries, per the brief.\n137|\n138|## Bounds of this review — what it cannot see\n139|\n140|- Reading-based. Nothing was executed except one 12-line Go probe of stdlib JSON surrogate behavior. Race conditions, fault-injection paths, and the fuzz corpora are unverified beyond their source.\n141|- Test files were read selectively (~30% of the 15.8k test lines): all inventory/census machinery, the §7.A and generation tests, conformance harness heads. Fixture-correctness of the remaining leaf tests is taken on the four review rounds.\n142|- Baseline packages (`canonicaljson`, `axerror`, `catalog`, etc.) were consulted at the seams only, not re-reviewed; `catalog_gen.go` was trusted against the spec.\n143|- Spec fidelity was checked only where the two Stories meet or where a divergence was suspected; this is not a clause-by-clause audit of §4 or §7.\n144|- The method finds disagreements between things that exist. It cannot find what both Stories consistently omitted, unless the spec or the catalog names it (that is how A3 was found; a shared omission the catalog also misses would be invisible here).\n145|\n146|## Summary for the producers\n147|\n148|| # | Severity | Where | One-line fix |\n149||---|----------|-------|--------------|\n150|| B1 | Blocking | `terminalbackend.go:557,568` | Add `TerminalBindingID` to `InstanceBinding` + check + negative test, or stated bound + gap entry |\n151|| B2 | Blocking | `terminalbackend.go:592`, `manifest.go:379,1328` | Count runes, not bytes; multibyte boundary fixtures at 256/257 |\n152|| B3 | Blocking | `terminalbackend/manifest.go:273`, `provhost/protocol.go:262` | Reuse/copy `canonicaljson.validateSurrogateEscapes`; one `\\ud800` refusal fixture each |\n153|| B4 | Blocking | `ownership.v0.5.0.json`, `traceability.go:43` | Register provider/provhost ownership, fix stale gap prose, re-pin hash; later add reverse scan |\n154|| A1 | Advisory | `canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go:115-132` vs `manifest.go:515` | Decide the one identity owner for terminal schemas |\n155|| A2 | Advisory | `provhost/identity.go:90` vs `core_records.go:207` | Delegate or agreement-test the duplicated §5.5 validator |\n156|| A3 | Advisory | `provhost/protocol.go:30`, catalog v3 pins | Name the Provider Protocol 3.0.0 owner; record the bound |\n157|| A4 | Advisory | `provider/provider.go:77-97` | Restructure provider errors for axerror composition (or document the lift contract) |\n158|| A5-A9 | Advisory | see above | Cross-package equality test; shared AST-walker core; trust-asymmetry comment; merge logistics; nits |","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
